Unit 1: The Homestead Pantry Audit (Applied Math & Economics)
Core Objective: Real-world mathematics and resource management using the household pantry as a living laboratory [cite: 2].
Level A: Core (Grades 5–8 & High School) [cite: 2]
Step 1: Ingest & Inventory — Audit and log physical counts, food types (grains, preserves, canned goods), and package weights [cite: 2].
Step 2: Unit Cost Calculations — Compute price-per-ounce or price-per-gram for five bulk items to find the best value [cite: 2].
Step 3: Proportion Analysis — Plot category ratios (e.g., dry goods vs. home preserves) onto a visual pie chart [cite: 2].
Step 4: Cost Savings Projections — Identify a wasted store-bought item and calculate the precise annual savings of replacing it with a home-grown, pressure-canned alternative [cite: 2].
Level B: Adapted (Grades 2–4)
Step 1: Container Hunt — Count pantry items up to 50. Group them into three simple visual categories: tin cans, cardboard boxes, and glass jars.
Step 2: Weight Sort — Locate the "Net Weight" on three different packages. Read the numbers and arrange the items from lightest to heaviest.
Step 3: Block Graphing — Draw a block graph on grid paper where each square equals one item to compare physical quantities visually.
Step 4: Smart Snacking — Track weekly wasted snacks. Use basic subtraction to subtract wasted items from the total purchased to count what was eaten.
Level C: Adapted (Grades K–2)
Step 1: Color & Shape Match — Group pantry items by container color (e.g., green cans, yellow boxes) or geometric shapes (cylinders vs. rectangular boxes).
Step 2: Counting Walk — Count items in each pile aloud up to 10 or 20, physically touching each item to build one-to-one counting skills.
Step 3: Picture Graphing — Draw simple shapes representing counts (e.g., drawing 4 circles for cans, 6 squares for boxes) on a drawing pad.
Step 4: Plant-to-Pantry Story — Verbally share how fruits and vegetables grow on farms, ending with drawing a picture of their favorite snack.
Unit 2: Rainwater Catchment & Filtration (Earth Science & Lab Physics)
Core Objective: Build and analyze a low-cost gravity water filter using gravel, sand, and charcoal to study earth sciences, fluid dynamics, and filtration [cite: 3].
Level A: Core (High School & Adult Path) [cite: 3]
Step 1: Physical Design — Calculate fluid volume, establish proper layer heights (gravel-to-sand ratio), and optimize flow rates [cite: 3].
Step 2: Budgeting Limits — Source components keeping the total setup cost under $20 (using recycled buckets and natural materials) [cite: 3].
Step 3: Sediment & Clarity Lab — Filter turbid (muddy) roof runoff [cite: 4]. Visually document the transition to clear, sediment-free water [cite: 4].
Step 4: Non-Potable Assay — Analyze pH compatibility for organic garden irrigation and non-drinking livestock watering uses [cite: 4].
Level B: Adapted (Grades 2–4)
Step 1: Rain Gauge Diary — Place a straight-sided clear glass jar outdoors during a rainstorm and measure rainfall height using a standard ruler.
Step 2: Earth Material Sorting — Touch and sort the filter ingredients into three glass jars: big rough rocks (gravel), tiny loose pieces (sand), and black flakes (charcoal).
Step 3: Clear Bottle Observation — Watch an adult pour muddy water through a plastic bottle filter. Draw a "before" and "after" picture showing how the water looks.
Step 4: The Plant Drink — Help water garden beds or greenhouse plants using the caught water, recording how moisture fuels growth.
Level C: Adapted (Grades K–2)
Step 1: Wet vs. Dry Sensory Play — Explore dry sand and dry pebbles. Pour water over them and verbally describe how they change in weight, color, and texture.
Step 2: Sink or Float Mystery — Drop a pebble, a leaf, and a piece of charcoal into a cup of water to observe which ones float at the surface and which sink.
Step 3: Visual Sifting Fun — Pour water containing twigs and grass over a kitchen colander to watch how the mesh traps large debris while letting clean water pass through.
Step 4: Rainwalk Discovery — Explore the homestead yard after a rainstorm. Search for puddles, mud patches, and earthworms to discover where the water went.
